# Advisory Floor

You cannot invoke a Claude skill or a live advisor from inside this dispatch.
When one of the triggers below fires, you cannot pause the world and wait for
a second opinion — so instead you SURFACE the escalation: proceed with your
best judgment, but make the decision and its uncertainty visible in your
final report / PR body inside a block marked exactly:

```
ADVISORY: <ADV.n>
Decision: <one sentence — the specific choice>
Options: A: <tradeoff> | B: <tradeoff> ...
Lean: <the option you took> — <source of uncertainty>
Context: <files, constraints, prior attempts>
```

A human or a stronger actor reviewing the PR can then re-run the advisory step
on that block. Do not silently pick an option and say nothing — the failure
this exists to catch is exactly a fork made at implementation speed with no
trace left behind.

## When to raise an ADVISORY block

- **ADV.1 — Architecture fork.** Two or more approaches require different data
  models, deployment topologies, or interface contracts to support future
  work. Ask: fast-forward one year — does today's choice block or complicate
  a plausible next capability? If the answer differs by option, this fires.
- **ADV.2 — Infrastructure change with live blast radius.** Any change to a
  system serving live traffic, production data, or gating access to either:
  deploy configs, DNS, certs, storage policy, network rules, platform
  routing. Sandboxed/dev-only changes with no live traffic do NOT trigger
  this.
- **ADV.3 — Structural boundary rework.** A change to a public interface,
  module boundary, or data contract that something *outside* the module must
  be updated, re-deployed, or re-tested to accommodate — regardless of line
  count. A large refactor confined inside one module with no external
  contract touched does NOT trigger this.
- **ADV.4 — Stuck on reasoning.** The same root cause has failed to resolve
  twice, and both failures were logic failures (evidence was complete, the
  conclusion was wrong) — not capacity failures (evidence was missing). Do
  not try a third theory from the same evidence; raise the block instead.
- **ADV.5 — Irreversible and ambiguous.** The action is hard to undo within
  one session (rebuilding data, re-migrating a schema, a rollback that has
  never been tested) AND the right path is genuinely unclear. Either
  condition alone does not qualify — a hard-to-reverse action with an
  obvious right answer just needs care, not an ADVISORY block.
- **Evidence-aware escalation (routing contract).** If you are being routed
  as a cheap/fast actor for this task-shape and have no track record — no
  prior evidence either way — for it, raise an ADVISORY block flagging that
  the routing choice itself is unverified, rather than silently proceeding
  on an untested assumption. Conversely, if you have a consistent track
  record on this exact task-shape, you do NOT need to raise a block just
  because the shape is unfamiliar in the abstract.

If unsure whether a trigger applies, raise the block anyway — the cost of an
unneeded one is a few lines in a PR body; the cost of a missed one is the
decision it would have caught.

## What NOT to do

Do not stop and wait for a response that will never come — this is a
non-interactive dispatch. Do not omit the block to save space. Do not bury the
decision in prose; use the exact `ADVISORY:` marker so it is greppable.

